00:09:03.773 --> 00:09:06.960
- God, I hate my eyes, so
let me ask you this, then,

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.850
are there any animals that
see outside of the spectrum?

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:12.970
- Oh, yeah.
- Deer.

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:13.803
- Deer?

00:09:13.803 --> 00:09:14.890
- Deer can see a little
bit in ultraviolet.

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:15.723
- A little ultraviolet?

00:09:15.723 --> 00:09:17.332
- Insects are all about ultraviolet.

00:09:17.332 --> 00:09:18.540
- Insects, bumblebees, yeah.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.790
- Cool, bumblebees see
in ultraviolet, as well.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:21.747
- Yup, a little bit.

00:09:21.747 --> 00:09:25.010
- And you know that they
like ultraviolet, okay,

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.570
this is how we know that we
are smarter than insects.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:28.403
- [Chuck] Okay.

00:09:28.403 --> 00:09:30.250
- Okay, evidence we are
smarter than insects.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:33.817
- All right, cool, no one is
stepping on us to kill us?

00:09:33.817 --> 00:09:34.733
No, go ahead.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:38.633
- We invented bug zappers,

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:42.837
which are intense in
ultraviolet, and they say,

00:09:42.837 --> 00:09:45.960
"I can't stop it," zap.
- Exactly.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.500
- And that's how we know
we're smarter than they are.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.250
And bug bulbs which you
don't see much anymore

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:55.220
'cause they're kind of
amber, they don't repel bugs,

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:59.720
bugs don't see it, so
their entire sensitivity

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:03.350
to light is shifted towards the blue end

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:04.610
into the ultraviolet,

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:07.960
and it dangles off the other
end some red and orange.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:10.130
So it's not that they avoid the red bulb

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:11.980
over your picnic table,
they don't see it at all.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:12.813
- They can't see it at all.

00:10:12.813 --> 00:10:15.690
- Yeah, and if you put a bug
zapper at the end of the lawn,

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:18.071
they all go to the bug
zapper, so I just want you

00:10:18.071 --> 00:10:21.310
to appreciate that we are
smarter than the bugs.

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:23.190
- So the alien invasion
comes and it's bugs

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:24.744
we should just rely on
our zappers, is that--

00:10:24.744 --> 00:10:26.280
- Oh good, good.
(overlapping chattering)

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:27.610
- If they are bugs.

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:30.010
- Yeah, but you know what,
if an alien invasion,

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:32.250
if they were smart enough to get here

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:34.270
and we're too stupid to leave here,

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:36.500
something tells me they're
gonna have the equivalent

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:40.840
of a bug zapper for us, like
a concert where, you know,

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:42.692
just like, "oh my god, a free concert"--

00:10:42.692 --> 00:10:44.460
- Free concert and good food.
- Right, exactly.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.441
Free concert and hamburgers, what?

00:10:46.441 --> 00:10:47.274
- Right.
(overlapping chattering)

00:10:47.274 --> 00:10:48.245
- You know, we walk in and zap, right.

00:10:48.245 --> 00:10:49.278
(overlapping chattering)

00:10:49.278 --> 00:10:50.111
- And no one comes out
to tell you not to go.

00:10:50.111 --> 00:10:51.740
- Exactly, 'cause everybody got
zapped when they were there.

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:53.560
Dude, that must be the best concert ever,

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.220
it's been going on for six
days and nobody's come out.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:57.850
Yeah.
- That wouldn't be fun.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:00.240
- Yeah, we would be putty in
the hands of a smarter species,

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:02.600
for sure, all right, next question.

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:05.730
- All right, here we go,
Pintibot on Instagram wants

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:07.507
to know this, "Since you guys were talking

00:11:07.507 --> 00:11:09.707
"about the different types
of light in the spectrum,

00:11:09.707 --> 00:11:13.267
"what is the difference between
Chandra X-ray Observatory

00:11:13.267 --> 00:11:17.140
"and James Webb Telescope,"
is it just the spectrum

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:20.280
of light, or what else do
they do that is different?

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:21.540
- Let's start out with the orbit,

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:23.880
so what orbit is Chandra in?

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.390
- So Chandra is in a
highly elliptical orbit

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:27.420
that goes about a third
of the way to the moon.

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.040
Chandra is about the size of a school bus,

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:32.321
it weighs maybe 4800
Kilograms, something like that,

00:11:32.321 --> 00:11:33.154
and--
- On earth.

00:11:33.154 --> 00:11:35.930
- Yes, exactly, exactly,
very important detail.

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:37.390
- On the moon it weighs one sixth of that.

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:38.390
- Yeah, you know, actually
the interesting thing

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:41.150
about Chandra is it was,
I'm pretty sure still was

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.683
the heaviest thing that the
space shuttle ever launched.

00:11:43.683 --> 00:11:44.516
- [Neil] Really?

00:11:44.516 --> 00:11:47.295
- And the fact that it
was so massive meant that,

00:11:47.295 --> 00:11:49.810
and I didn't actually learn
this until many years later,

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:51.730
the fact that it was so massive meant

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:54.510
that it was a more risky
ride for the astronauts

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.668
than it would have been
with a lighter payload.

00:11:56.668 --> 00:11:57.501
- Right.

00:11:57.501 --> 00:12:01.180
- Their abort scenarios, for
example, were more challenging,

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:02.397
but I didn't know that at the time.

00:12:02.397 --> 00:12:04.690
So, yeah, so Chandra
was large and in charge,

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:07.202
and it was fortunately perfect--

00:12:07.202 --> 00:12:08.760
- Large and in charge.
- Everything went perfect

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:09.907
to get it up into space,
thanks to the astronauts--

00:12:09.907 --> 00:12:11.070
- And so that's that orbit,

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:15.780
and so James Webb is a million
miles on the other side

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:16.780
of the moon.
- Right.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:20.885
- So they want that away
from earth interference.

00:12:20.885 --> 00:12:21.718
- [Chuck] Right.

00:12:21.718 --> 00:12:22.551
- [Kim] Yeah.

00:12:22.551 --> 00:12:25.620
- So James Webb is an
infrared-based telescope, and again,

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:28.750
different parts of the spectrum
were tuned differently,

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:32.090
and so they have their
targets of interests,

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:35.510
their objects of affection in
the universe are different.

00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:36.740
But then you bring them all together,

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:38.030
and you get the full picture.

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:39.850
- So now you mentioned the orbits,

00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:43.840
this is a question coming
from Chuck Nice on Facebook.

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:45.150
(group laughs)

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:45.983
- Chuck Nice, in person.

00:12:45.983 --> 00:12:46.816
- Chuck Nice here in the office.

00:12:46.816 --> 00:12:48.730
- Oh, wait, let me just comment about,

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:50.720
I have to say something
more about James Webb.

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:51.720
- Go ahead.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:54.180
- So Chandra's in this
big elliptical orbit,

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:58.503
so it's orbiting earth James
Webb is in a Lagrangian point.

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:01.830
It is a point where all forces

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:04.220
that would otherwise move it are stable,

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:05.270
and so you put it out there,

00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:07.880
and it takes very little station keeping

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:11.630
to just keep it there, and this is the,

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.430
these famous Lagrangian
points are where we imagine

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:17.440
you'd build stuff 'cause you
can just get all your hardware,

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:18.720
and just load it there
- Just leave it there, right.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:20.470
- You leave it there--
- And just let it hang around.

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:21.303
- Just hang.
- Right.

00:13:21.303 --> 00:13:23.399
- It's the garage patch
of the solar system.

00:13:23.399 --> 00:13:24.847
- It's what it is, it'll collect it, if,

00:13:24.847 --> 00:13:27.300
oh, I need a bolt or a screw, there it is,

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:28.700
just floating right by.
- It just hangs right there.

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:30.450
- It doesn't fall to anybody's surface.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:33.310
So they were imagined to
be a little more useful

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:35.650
than they've turned out to be,
turns out we can make things

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:37.360
that are orbiting, it's not that hard,

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:38.670
'cause once you bring
something up into orbit,

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:39.930
it orbits with you.
- With you, right.

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:42.830
- Yeah, yeah, so it's not, but anyhow--

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:45.971
- Although I do love the
Lagrangian point, like,

00:13:45.971 --> 00:13:47.483
as a place, you know?
- It has cool name.

00:13:47.483 --> 00:13:49.360
- It's a total cool name.
- You know, right?

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:50.193
- Exactly.
- Lagrange.

00:13:50.193 --> 00:13:55.150
(overlapping chattering)
(group laughs)

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:56.500
- All right, so now let me ask you this.

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:57.624
- Okay.

00:13:57.624 --> 00:14:00.070
- As these orbits happen,

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:01.690
are they,

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:04.370
are they staying on a fixed point,

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:06.560
or are they observing different quadrants

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:08.227
as they move around?

00:14:08.227 --> 00:14:09.950
- So Chandra goes a third
of the way to the moon

00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:12.990
at its farthest point, and
then goes about 1600 kilometers

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:14.100
to earth at its closest point,

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:17.200
so it's this nice elliptical
orbit, and it's got,

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.900
they did that for, like,
optimal observing capabilities

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:21.430
so that it has the most time

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:23.306
to essentially be looking
out at the universe.

00:14:23.306 --> 00:14:24.350
- Gotcha.

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:25.183
- But, you know--
- Far away from earth.

00:14:25.183 --> 00:14:27.850
- Far away from earth, exactly.

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:30.560
But yeah, I think what's
really interesting about,

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:32.447
well, for one thing, I think
it must have gone, like,

00:14:32.447 --> 00:14:35.500
2.7 billion mile, I mean,
kilometers by now over 20 years,

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:37.080
which is, I think,
fantastic and I think about,

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:38.870
like, Chandra's never had a day off

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:41.280
in, like, 20 years doesn't
even have, like, an hour off--

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:43.617
- She works hard for the--
- Right, I know.

00:14:43.617 --> 00:14:45.930
And how perfect that had to
work when it was launched.

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:49.070
So anyway, I'm not sure what
James Webb is doing but--

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:50.730
- Right, so, well it's
not up yet, of course.

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:52.246
Right.
- True, when it goes.

00:14:52.246 --> 00:14:53.920
- At the time of this broadcast, but,

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:57.210
so these things have gyros
that enable you to know

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:58.910
where you are and where you're pointing

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:00.497
and so you give it, send
coordinates up there,

00:15:00.497 --> 00:15:04.200
and you pick out your object
of interest and gather data.

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:07.660
So there are some, I think
I understand the point

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:09.740
of that question, there
are some telescopes

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:11.160
that only observe one patch of sky.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:12.248
- [Chuck] Right, exactly.

00:15:12.248 --> 00:15:13.081
- [Kim] Sure, yes.

00:15:13.081 --> 00:15:14.270
- And it's hammer that
for, and they get better,

00:15:14.270 --> 00:15:17.150
deeper data, Kepler did that.
- Right.

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:19.700
- Kepler was one patch of
sky looking, there was a lot

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:22.410
of stars, but it was still one
patch looking for exoplanets.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:26.000
And, 'cause it had to go
back looking for variations

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:27.350
in the host star
- Right.

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:28.830
- So one set of data's not good enough,

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:30.390
you gotta go back and
back and back and then--

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:31.703
- [Chuck] And compare all
the different images--

00:15:31.703 --> 00:15:33.699
- Compare all the data,
right, right, right, so,

00:15:33.699 --> 00:15:36.300
and let me take this
moment, let's go over just

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:40.340
who these people are, okay,
so James Webb was the,

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:42.130
he was head of NASA during the 1960s.

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:42.963
- Cool.

00:15:42.963 --> 00:15:47.490
- Yeah, and, but he was, I
think he was the first person

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:50.440
who we named a telescope after
that was not a scientist,

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:51.439
so I think--
- What?

00:15:51.439 --> 00:15:52.710
- [Neil] There might have been
some political stuff going on

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.683
in the back room.
- That's kind of cool, though,

00:15:54.683 --> 00:15:55.516
I kind of like James Webb--
- And I think

00:15:55.516 --> 00:15:57.330
that was the first naming
before launch too right?

00:15:57.330 --> 00:15:59.580
- Oh yeah, yeah, normally
you name it after a person

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:02.507
after launch, just in case
it blows up or something.

00:16:02.507 --> 00:16:03.926
- Right.
- Bad luck.

00:16:03.926 --> 00:16:06.550
- Your name blows up with the thing.

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:08.400
- Chandra was AXAF for a long time

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:09.540
and AXAF--
- I forgot all about that.

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:11.255
- Doesn't quite roll off the tongue--

00:16:11.255 --> 00:16:12.130
- AXAF?

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:13.754
- Yeah, X-ray Astrophysics
Facility or something.

00:16:13.754 --> 00:16:14.974
- Advanced X-ray Astrophysics Facility--

00:16:14.974 --> 00:16:16.415
- Yeah, yeah, astrophysics, yeah, yeah.

00:16:16.415 --> 00:16:17.248
- But then it was renamed Chandra

00:16:17.248 --> 00:16:19.340
after Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar,

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:22.490
who was a very famous
Indian-American Nobel Laureate

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:24.972
who studied things like white
dwarfs and stuff like that,

00:16:24.972 --> 00:16:25.805
so--
- Very nice.

00:16:25.805 --> 00:16:28.534
- And he also did, I probably
have a book by Chandra,

00:16:28.534 --> 00:16:29.959
let me see here, hold on.

00:16:29.959 --> 00:16:31.660
- Oh, look at that.
- How funny.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:32.760
- There it is.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:34.700
- Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar.
- Yeah.

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:38.321
- Radiative Transfer, so one
of the more brilliant among us.

00:16:38.321 --> 00:16:41.130
- Okay, I'm through,
I can't even with you.

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:41.963
- What, what?

00:16:41.963 --> 00:16:42.796
- [Chuck] I'm just done with you.

00:16:42.796 --> 00:16:43.629
- What, what?

00:16:43.629 --> 00:16:44.462
- [Chuck] Like seriously

00:16:44.462 --> 00:16:46.270
- What, you've got issues?
- Yeah, I got issues

00:16:46.270 --> 00:16:47.790
'cause I'm not an, this is the crap,

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:48.853
not the crap--
- It is?

00:16:48.853 --> 00:16:49.840
- But this is what you're reading?

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:50.673
- Yes.

00:16:50.673 --> 00:16:51.506
- [Chuck] You're sitting
around reading this?

00:16:51.506 --> 00:16:52.339
Give me this for a second.
- No.

00:16:52.339 --> 00:16:54.170
- Give it here, give me
that book for a second.

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:55.350
- What?
- I can't believe that,

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:57.910
okay, people at home, I just--
- Open any page.

00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:00.620
- I'm just, wait, let me
just open it up, okay,

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:01.894
here's the page, I swear to God--

00:17:01.894 --> 00:17:03.022
- [Neil] Oh, you're gonna read us--

00:17:03.022 --> 00:17:04.182
- I'm gonna read.
- It's not light reading.

00:17:04.182 --> 00:17:05.081
- I'm gonna read it to you,
I'm gonna read it to you--

00:17:05.081 --> 00:17:05.914
- [Neil] Chuck's bed time stories

00:17:05.914 --> 00:17:08.477
from Radium Transfer
- Here it is, exactly.

00:17:08.477 --> 00:17:12.483
"Principles of invariance," what is that?

00:17:12.483 --> 00:17:13.330
What is that?
- A squiggly line. (laughs)

00:17:13.330 --> 00:17:17.504
- What is that, squiggly line,
squiggly line doodle sign.

00:17:17.504 --> 00:17:19.000
(Neil laughs)

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:20.213
And by the way--
- Okay, so--

00:17:20.213 --> 00:17:24.760
- This just goes on for page
after page after page of this.

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:29.640
Some of the pages are just
nothing but actual equations,

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:32.430
just after equation after equation.

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:35.690
I have seen Chinese
newspapers that are easier

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:37.870
to understand than this.
- All right, so Chuck,

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:38.703
if you write a book like this,

00:17:38.703 --> 00:17:40.214
you get a telescope named after you.

00:17:40.214 --> 00:17:41.328
(Chuck and Neil laughing)

00:17:41.328 --> 00:17:42.460
- That is true.
- Unbelievable.

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:43.463
- So he's wrote several books

00:17:43.463 --> 00:17:46.846
that really were the definitive
word on those subjects,

00:17:46.846 --> 00:17:48.010
and they're still used in graduate school.

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:49.660
- Yeah, and it was a naming contest.

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:51.720
We actually had a contest for the naming

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:53.570
and it was a teacher and
a high school student

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:55.617
that picked the name Chandra
as the winning entry.

00:17:55.617 --> 00:17:56.450
- Oh, very cool.
- See, they knew.

00:17:56.450 --> 00:17:59.100
- So yeah, they knew, they
did some excellent research

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.640
and did not mind the equations.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:02.710
- So we've gotta take a break.
- Okay.

