00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:44.440
that makes a radio wave
that is the diameter

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:47.963
of earth's orbit around the
sun, who's detecting that?

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:51.800
So there could be
phenomena in the universe

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:55.210
that is washing across
the entire solar system

00:27:55.210 --> 00:27:57.610
and we don't have detectors
that can pick it up.

00:27:58.530 --> 00:28:00.490
- Wow.
- I do like radio waves.

00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:02.047
If I had to pick a second
favorite beside X-rays,

00:28:02.047 --> 00:28:03.170
I think it would be radio waves.

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:04.590
- [Neil] That's a nice
pair of waves right there.

00:28:04.590 --> 00:28:07.110
- Yeah, they really are,
they're really complimentary.

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:08.610
It's true, even though
they're on opposite parts.

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:10.019
- They're opposite
parts, but they're good.

00:28:10.019 --> 00:28:11.649
- But opposites attract
I mean it really does--

00:28:11.649 --> 00:28:14.030
- Plus they're highly used
in our culture, radio waves

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:15.702
for communication, and
X-rays for medicine.

00:28:15.702 --> 00:28:16.535
- X-rays for health.
- Health, yeah.

00:28:16.535 --> 00:28:17.380
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:19.101
- Very, very cool.
- All right.

00:28:19.101 --> 00:28:23.573
- All right, could there
be a, oh, sorry, Julie H,

00:28:24.490 --> 00:28:29.170
who comes to us @TimeTraveling
on Twitter, I like that,

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:31.767
She says, "Could there be
technology like Street View

00:28:31.767 --> 00:28:34.457
"on Google Maps that
visits various points,

00:28:34.457 --> 00:28:37.070
"just points in the universe?"

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:38.820
- Oh, that's a great question.
- Interesting question, right?

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:41.120
- Yeah, so we have

00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:45.372
Google Mars, I think it's
called, you can look that up,

00:28:45.372 --> 00:28:48.370
GoogleMars.com, or maybe it's
Mars.Google.com, whatever,

00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:52.460
that it's almost like Street
View of some of the data

00:28:52.460 --> 00:28:54.670
on Mars, and that's really amazing,

00:28:54.670 --> 00:28:58.540
getting more three dimensional,
which I think is the point

00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:00.310
she's kind of getting at there.

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:02.230
Data of our universe is really hard.

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:05.680
Once you're going beyond nearby
objects in the solar system,

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:07.760
and you're going farther
out past the stars,

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:09.450
it's really hard to get some sort

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:11.690
of usable dimensional
data on that to then turn

00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:15.430
into a 3D model that you can
tour in like a street view type

00:29:15.430 --> 00:29:16.754
of map.
- So you think,

00:29:16.754 --> 00:29:19.230
you don't do 3D modeling for Chandra?

00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:20.320
- We do.
- You do?

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:23.049
- It's just hard. (laughs)
- Oh, okay, okay.

00:29:23.049 --> 00:29:24.790
- But we do, yeah, actually,

00:29:24.790 --> 00:29:26.307
I do have a 3D model here.
- We do things not

00:29:26.307 --> 00:29:28.570
because they're easy--
- But because they're hard.

00:29:28.570 --> 00:29:30.570
- So you brought to show and tell.

00:29:30.570 --> 00:29:33.000
- I did bring something for
show and tell because, again,

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:34.430
I'm a very visual person.
- This is an audio show.

00:29:34.430 --> 00:29:36.570
- Which does not help
the audio folks, I know.

00:29:36.570 --> 00:29:38.110
- Well it, but it--
- But we can describe it.

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:42.607
- Yeah, so it is a kind of
globular looking structure,

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:47.890
and it has many different
nodes that are jutting out

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:48.723
from it, and--

00:29:48.723 --> 00:29:50.059
- It looks like a tumor
removed from somebody's body.

00:29:50.059 --> 00:29:51.161
- It really does.
- Yeah, so,

00:29:51.161 --> 00:29:53.364
but there's a reason for that. (laughs)

00:29:53.364 --> 00:29:54.905
- If you've ever--
- You know what it looks like?

00:29:54.905 --> 00:29:58.600
It looks like a fossilized,
no, calcified coral,

00:29:58.600 --> 00:29:59.759
that's what that looks like.
- Oh, very good.

00:29:59.759 --> 00:30:00.592
- Yes, yes, it does, yes.
- That good?

00:30:00.592 --> 00:30:01.741
- Yes, so--
- Wow, Neil.

00:30:01.741 --> 00:30:02.960
That was excellent.
- Why it looks biological,

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:04.577
though, it's because we actually use--

00:30:04.577 --> 00:30:05.410
- [Neil] Why it's what, you say?

00:30:05.410 --> 00:30:06.920
- Why it looks biological, it's

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:10.530
because we actually used
brain imaging software adapted

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:13.920
from some local area brain
scientists in the Boston area

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:15.750
to make it, that, we used their software.

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:17.930
So that's why it looks more brainy ish

00:30:17.930 --> 00:30:20.030
or biological ish than you
would probably expect otherwise.

00:30:20.030 --> 00:30:24.210
- That's funny because before
we sat down I picked this up

00:30:24.210 --> 00:30:27.520
and I said, "Is this
like a firing neuron?"

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:30.330
- Right, right, right.
(overlapping chattering)

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:32.170
Yes, well, I mean, if you look at visuals

00:30:32.170 --> 00:30:33.400
from the micro versus the macro,

00:30:33.400 --> 00:30:34.630
now you're speaking my language 'cause

00:30:34.630 --> 00:30:36.760
of my biology background, but you can see

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:38.150
so many similarities in the way

00:30:38.150 --> 00:30:41.000
that you process those data, right?

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:43.710
But what you're holding is
a 3D model of Cassiopeia A,

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:45.800
our good friend that we
were talking about earlier,

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:47.457
the supernova remnant.
- That's so cool.

00:30:47.457 --> 00:30:50.100
- Yeah, 10, 11,000 light years away,

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:52.150
and you're able to hold
a version in your hand,

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:53.850
essentially because of
the Doppler effect, right?

00:30:53.850 --> 00:30:56.260
- So did you 3D print that?
- Yeah, this is 3D printed.

00:30:56.260 --> 00:30:58.320
- Okay.
- Yep, so we worked with--

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:00.290
- So the Doppler effect
gives you depth information?

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:01.600
- Right, exactly.
- Okay.

00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:03.840
- So Tracey DeLaney, she was
the scientist who first worked

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:05.640
on this, she was at MIT
at the time, and she was

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:07.930
essentially figuring out what
information was moving away

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:08.927
and what was moving towards--

00:31:08.927 --> 00:31:10.818
- Was she with their
imaging lab, MIT has a big

00:31:10.818 --> 00:31:12.270
and famous imaging lab.
- No, she wasn't actually.

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:14.010
This was separate, but we hooked her up

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:15.710
with the folks who were working

00:31:15.710 --> 00:31:18.310
on the medical imaging
software translation,

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:21.470
which was called
Astronomical Medicine, and--

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:23.130
- That's cool, I like that.
- And this was the result.

00:31:23.130 --> 00:31:25.990
- I like that hybridization.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:27.550
- This is fascinating, I love it.

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:29.260
- So scale is an issue, though, obviously,

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:31.070
when you're holding something
that small, this is,

00:31:31.070 --> 00:31:33.170
like, four inches across
for those listening, maybe--

00:31:33.170 --> 00:31:34.570
- Okay, what we can do,
maybe we'll photograph it,

00:31:34.570 --> 00:31:36.917
and we'll post it next to the audio.

00:31:36.917 --> 00:31:39.250
- But in real life, it's
like, the surface area is

00:31:39.250 --> 00:31:41.210
maybe 40 million billion
times the surface area

00:31:41.210 --> 00:31:43.050
of our sun and planets,
and you can toss in Pluto,

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:44.460
if you like, it doesn't matter.

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:45.900
- Yeah, yeah, Pluto, yeah, or toss it out,

00:31:45.900 --> 00:31:48.060
it doesn't matter.
(Kim laughs)

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:48.893
- But, yeah, so 3D is hard--

00:31:48.893 --> 00:31:50.291
- I buried my hatchet with Pluto,

00:31:50.291 --> 00:31:51.743
me and Pluto are good.
- Oh, good, good, good.

00:31:53.410 --> 00:31:55.030
- All right, Chuck?
- Only you have

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:56.373
to bury the hatchet with--
- Pluto.

00:31:56.373 --> 00:31:59.650
- With that dwarf planet,
I was about to say planet.

00:31:59.650 --> 00:32:01.860
- With a hound dog.
- Yeah, yes, exactly

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:03.810
- Blood hound, I think it is.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:05.020
All right, do we have time for one more?

00:32:05.020 --> 00:32:06.790
- Well, let me hear, let
me hear the question.

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:08.190
- All right, I'm gonna
give you the question,

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:10.197
this is Tom Rex from Facebook, he says,

00:32:10.197 --> 00:32:11.907
"Do you think virtual reality will

00:32:11.907 --> 00:32:13.517
"ever allow the human brain to

00:32:13.517 --> 00:32:16.957
"completely comprehend
the immense distances

00:32:16.957 --> 00:32:19.177
"between planets, stars, and galaxies,

00:32:19.177 --> 00:32:22.560
"or is this something that
we'll never fully grasp?"

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:25.350
- And we will answer
that after this break.

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:26.318
- Nice.
- See what I did there?

00:32:26.318 --> 00:32:27.151
(overlapping chattering)

00:32:27.151 --> 00:32:29.539
- It's called a tease.
- It's called a tease.

00:32:29.539 --> 00:32:30.372
(overlapping chattering)

00:32:30.372 --> 00:32:32.720
This is StarTalk Cosmic Queries,

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:36.557
X-ray astrophysics edition,
we'll see you in a moment.

00:32:37.413 --> 00:32:42.000
- StarTalk Cosmic Queries
X-ray astrophysics edition.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:43.530
We're celebrating the 20th Anniversary

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:46.803
of the launch of the
Chandra X-ray telescope,

00:32:46.803 --> 00:32:49.310
one of the great observatories, up there

00:32:49.310 --> 00:32:52.290
with Hubble and James
Webb and the rest of them,

00:32:52.290 --> 00:32:56.430
each of them targeting their
window to the universe.

00:32:56.430 --> 00:32:57.263
- Nice.

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:01.070
- I got Kim, Kim Arcand.
- Hello.

00:33:01.070 --> 00:33:02.290
- Yes.

00:33:02.290 --> 00:33:04.403
- And Chuck.
- Yes.

00:33:04.403 --> 00:33:06.840
- So we left off, we left people dangling,

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:11.140
where, can virtual reality
help us comprehend the scale

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:13.070
of sizes and distances and things?

00:33:13.070 --> 00:33:16.537
Let's make this a more broad
visualization question.

00:33:16.537 --> 00:33:17.490
- [Chuck] All right.

00:33:17.490 --> 00:33:20.420
- Par of you job, Kim, is to get people

00:33:20.420 --> 00:33:22.750
to see things we don't otherwise see,

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:26.450
or to grasp scale and
texture and phenomenon

00:33:26.450 --> 00:33:29.570
that is not otherwise
accessible to us looking

00:33:29.570 --> 00:33:31.460
on our Instagram account, so

00:33:33.630 --> 00:33:37.740
what role do you see that
you play in getting us closer

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:40.050
to the universe?
- Oh, that's a great question.

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:42.950
I think mostly my job is to just sort

00:33:42.950 --> 00:33:46.400
of oversee all the
various visual platforms

00:33:46.400 --> 00:33:49.980
that we can take Chandra
data to, I mean, we--

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:50.980
- [Neil] Oh, it's not just photographs?

00:33:50.980 --> 00:33:53.963
- Not just images, yeah, I mean, Chandra,

00:33:54.836 --> 00:33:55.890
one of the great things
when you have a telescope

00:33:55.890 --> 00:33:57.080
that's been up there for so long is

00:33:57.080 --> 00:33:59.750
you just have a fantastic
archive of data to work with

00:33:59.750 --> 00:34:02.710
and as technology has
developed in other sectors,

00:34:02.710 --> 00:34:05.400
you have all of these new
platforms to try it out with.

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:06.967
So we were talking about
3D printing earlier,

00:34:06.967 --> 00:34:08.910
and the idea of what
you do with 3D models--

00:34:08.910 --> 00:34:10.167
- So you gotta stay current with all that.

00:34:10.167 --> 00:34:11.000
- You do.

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:12.950
- And exploit it in it's service of--

00:34:12.950 --> 00:34:15.130
- Yeah, when I started
working for Chandra,

00:34:15.130 --> 00:34:17.670
Cassiopeia A was one of the
first objects we ever looked at,

00:34:17.670 --> 00:34:20.610
right, and it was beautiful in
a flat two dimensional image

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:23.060
and I was amazed, never
would I have imagined,

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:25.260
fast forward 20 years, and
I'm holding a version of it

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:28.110
in my hand, or walking
inside it in virtual reality.

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:33.110
Like, those technologies were
not a reality at the time,

00:34:33.200 --> 00:34:35.670
so with things like virtual
reality, or augmented reality,

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:38.870
mixed reality, data
sonification using sound,

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:40.053
there are all of these ways to take that--

00:34:40.053 --> 00:34:41.660
- Data sonification?
- Yes.

00:34:41.660 --> 00:34:44.330
- So add another sense
to the interpretation

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:45.307
of the data.
- So, for example--

00:34:45.307 --> 00:34:46.140
- Is this also good for blind people?

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:48.500
- Exactly, Dr. Wanda Diaz
actually has done a lot

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:50.240
of work around that, my kind

00:34:50.240 --> 00:34:53.120
of perfect world would be a
virtual reality application

00:34:53.120 --> 00:34:55.810
where you have the visual,
of course, but then

00:34:55.810 --> 00:34:59.250
you have the layer of sound
that's also spatially attached,

00:34:59.250 --> 00:35:01.460
so you know where things
are, and then also, like,

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:03.851
a haptic layer, so you can
actually feel vibrations.

00:35:03.851 --> 00:35:04.684
- Haptic?
- Touch.

00:35:04.684 --> 00:35:05.742
- So, like, you know,
when your phone vibrates--

00:35:05.742 --> 00:35:06.941
- Why didn't you just say touch?

00:35:06.941 --> 00:35:08.573
- 'Cause haptic is much a cooler word.

00:35:08.573 --> 00:35:11.470
- I know, it's called haptic
technology, that's what we use,

00:35:11.470 --> 00:35:13.370
I don't know, but yes,
essentially by being able

00:35:13.370 --> 00:35:15.340
to feel those vibrations as you're moving

00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:17.030
through the remnant, right?

00:35:17.030 --> 00:35:18.160
So there are all these applications,

00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:19.830
none of which were around--

00:35:19.830 --> 00:35:20.930
- So moving through the remnant,

00:35:20.930 --> 00:35:25.860
so you have the 3D model
and you become a journey,

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:29.040
you journey through the model?
- Exactly, exploring it.

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:30.140
Now the scale is still hard, right--

00:35:30.140 --> 00:35:30.973
- Oh my God, they used to have

00:35:30.973 --> 00:35:32.610
that at the Franklin
Institute, it was a heart.

00:35:32.610 --> 00:35:34.753
- Yeah, oh, right, yeah, yes, correct.

00:35:34.753 --> 00:35:35.586
- And I remember--
- The living heart.

00:35:35.586 --> 00:35:37.070
- The living heart, you
would walk through the heart.

00:35:37.070 --> 00:35:39.300
- Right, so think of that,
except virtual, right?

00:35:39.300 --> 00:35:42.060
But then having cues of sound and touch,

00:35:42.060 --> 00:35:44.800
and it's a wholly different understanding

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:46.620
and experience of that information.

00:35:46.620 --> 00:35:48.710
Now, going back to the question of scale,

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:51.230
I mean, as soon as you get
out of earth-size scale

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:53.240
and even smaller than
that, it's really hard

00:35:53.240 --> 00:35:57.570
for human understanding and
relations of what we know.

00:35:57.570 --> 00:36:00.770
So whether that will actually
help people understand

00:36:00.770 --> 00:36:02.880
and comprehend some of these
vast scales, I don't know,

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:03.850
but it helps--

00:36:03.850 --> 00:36:05.410
- It might help, but here's the thing,

00:36:05.410 --> 00:36:08.480
it's just not, everything that we see,

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:11.326
we imagine on the scale that we see it.

00:36:11.326 --> 00:36:12.159
- Right, exactly.

00:36:12.159 --> 00:36:14.150
- Because that's the scale
in which we live, and so--

00:36:14.150 --> 00:36:17.130
- The scale in which
our senses were forged.

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:18.150
- Correct, yes.
- Exactly.

00:36:18.150 --> 00:36:21.430
- So the problem is that
even if you were able

00:36:21.430 --> 00:36:24.460
to demonstrate it, your
brain would be resistant

00:36:24.460 --> 00:36:27.590
to actually then
re-visualizing it that way

00:36:27.590 --> 00:36:29.820
because you're so used
to looking at, you know,

00:36:29.820 --> 00:36:31.930
the world through the eyes that you have,

00:36:31.930 --> 00:36:34.883
which is, like, Neil does
this thing where he shows,

00:36:35.720 --> 00:36:39.250
which the first time I saw
it, 'cause he did it for me,

00:36:39.250 --> 00:36:41.130
I was like, "Get out of here,"

00:36:41.130 --> 00:36:44.510
and he showed me just where the moon

00:36:44.510 --> 00:36:48.230
and the sun and the earth
are and it was, like--

00:36:48.230 --> 00:36:49.537
- Relative to each other.
- Relative to each other,

00:36:49.537 --> 00:36:52.370
and we just did it with a basketball

00:36:52.370 --> 00:36:54.860
and something else, and we
did it in a regular room,

00:36:54.860 --> 00:36:56.070
and I'm like, "You gotta be kidding me."

00:36:56.070 --> 00:37:00.370
You know what I mean, so even
being able to see it in--

00:37:00.370 --> 00:37:04.483
- And those are just planets.
(overlapping chattering)

00:37:05.489 --> 00:37:06.510
- We're now talking about galaxies.

00:37:06.510 --> 00:37:07.990
- Exactly, yeah.
- That's crazy.

00:37:07.990 --> 00:37:09.910
- So, yeah, I don't think
the human brain could

00:37:09.910 --> 00:37:13.430
ever comprehend those vast
scales, it's just too much.

00:37:13.430 --> 00:37:14.310
- Cool.
- Yeah.

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:16.530
- Oh, man, this is good stuff--

00:37:16.530 --> 00:37:18.520
- I got another quick one,
just while we're there.

00:37:18.520 --> 00:37:19.430
- Yeah.

00:37:19.430 --> 00:37:21.660
- You might ask, you see all
these stars in these photos,

00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:23.570
and you say, will stars ever collide?

00:37:23.570 --> 00:37:26.940
Well, they do, rarely,
but they do and they can,

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:28.740
and it's interesting when that happens,

00:37:28.740 --> 00:37:31.790
but to appreciate how rare that is,

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:34.410
if there were four bumblebees

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:36.773
in the United States flying randomly,

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:39.080
there's a higher chance that two of them

